WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:10.722
Hi! Press a switch and the light comes on. How does electricity make a bulb light up? Let's learn about electric circuits.

2
00:00:10.872 --> 00:00:17.754
A simple circuit has a few parts. The battery, also called a cell, provides the electricity.

3
00:00:17.904 --> 00:00:22.050
Wires connect the parts and let the electric current flow.

4
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.530
The bulb lights up when current flows through it.

5
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:32.706
The switch opens or closes the circuit, so it controls whether the bulb is on or off.

6
00:00:32.856 --> 00:00:42.378
When the switch is closed, the current flows from the battery, through the wires and the bulb, and back to the battery in a complete loop. This is a closed circuit.

7
00:00:42.528 --> 00:00:47.418
Current flows only in a closed circuit, and only then does the bulb light up.

8
00:00:47.568 --> 00:00:56.250
If we open the switch, there is a gap in the circuit. This is an open circuit. No current can flow, so the bulb doesn't light.

9
00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:02.058
A loose wire or a blown bulb also makes an open circuit.

10
00:01:02.208 --> 00:01:14.370
Scientists draw circuits using simple symbols. This is called a circuit diagram. The symbol for a cell is a long line and a short line. The long line is the positive end.

11
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.666
The symbol for a bulb is a circle with a cross inside.

12
00:01:18.816 --> 00:01:21.834
An open switch is drawn with a gap.

13
00:01:21.984 --> 00:01:26.034
Wires are drawn as straight lines.

14
00:01:26.184 --> 00:01:32.106
If a bulb doesn't light, check things one by one. First, is the switch closed?

15
00:01:32.256 --> 00:01:36.930
Second, are all the wires connected properly, or is one loose?

16
00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.914
Third, is the battery flat? Is the bulb blown?

17
00:01:41.064 --> 00:01:46.242
Just one gap anywhere in the circuit stops the current.

18
00:01:46.392 --> 00:01:53.634
Watch out! Some pupils think one wire from the battery to the bulb is enough to light it. That's wrong!

19
00:01:53.784 --> 00:01:59.922
The current leaves the battery, passes through the bulb, and must return to the other end of the battery.

20
00:02:00.072 --> 00:02:05.034
So the circuit must be a complete loop with no gaps.

21
00:02:05.184 --> 00:02:14.874
Your turn! The switch in the circuit is open. Will the bulb light? Pause and think.

22
00:02:15.024 --> 00:02:23.058
No. An open switch makes an open circuit, so no current flows. Did you get it right?

23
00:02:23.208 --> 00:02:33.738
Another one! In a circuit diagram, what does the long line in the cell symbol stand for? Pause and think.

24
00:02:33.888 --> 00:02:41.418
The long line is the positive end, and the short line is the negative end. Did you get it right?

25
00:02:41.568 --> 00:02:52.362
Last one! The switch is closed, but the bulb still doesn't light. What could be wrong? Pause and think.

26
00:02:52.512 --> 00:02:59.706
There might be a loose wire, a flat battery, or a blown bulb. Did you get it right?

27
00:02:59.856 --> 00:03:13.026
Remember: a circuit has a battery, wires, a bulb and a switch. Current flows only around a complete loop, a closed circuit. If there's a gap, it's an open circuit and the bulb won't light.

28
00:03:13.176 --> 00:03:19.266
Stay safe with electricity: never touch plugs with wet hands!

